// theater.marco — putting on a production: acting on the things a play means.
//
// # What a Target is, and why it names no provider
//
// A TARGET is what somebody meant to interact with: the Mouse button. It is not a UIA element, a
// DOM node, a rectangle or a detection. Those are how Marco FOUND it on one machine at one
// moment, and they belong to whichever provider did the finding.
//
//	Targets describe what the audience meant to interact with.
//	Providers describe how Marco found it.
//
// That split is the whole reason this act exists. A play written against a provider stops working
// when the provider does; a play written against a target keeps working wherever Marco can still
// recognise the thing, because the sentence says what it wants rather than where to look.
//
// # Why the Theater performs it
//
// Because deciding HOW is not the play's job and not the Director's. The Director says which
// production to put on; the Theater knows the stage it is standing on, which actors are available
// to it tonight, and casts one that can play the part. A play that chose its own actor would be a
// script with the casting notes written into the dialogue.
//
// # Why this is not part of Screen
//
// `screen.marco` says in its first paragraph that every capability in it READS, and that deciding
// must not be doing. Asking "is the settings screen up?" and pressing something on it are
// opposite kinds of sentence, and putting them in one act would quietly retire a promise that
// every guarded play depends on.
//
// # And why it is not part of Accessibility
//
// Because naming a provider in a play is the thing being fixed. The Accessibility act keeps its
// own capabilities for callers that genuinely hold a control already — see accessibility.marco,
// whose Control carries the source's own ephemeral id. That act is one of the actors this one
// may cast, not a synonym for it.
//
// See [[ADR-068-the-theater-is-the-durable-semantic-world]].

the Theater is an act.

this exports Activate.  // do to this target what pressing it would do

// A thing in the world, named the way a person would name it.
//
// Name is what the target is CALLED — the word on the button, as somebody reading the play would
// say it. It is a REFERENCE, not a search: Marco already knows this target, and the name is how
// the play refers to what Marco knows, exactly as `Screen's Showing with "the pause menu"` refers
// to a place. The evidence that resolves it lives with the target, not in the play.
//
// Kind is what sort of thing to expect — button, item, field, menu, link, tab, checkbox, window.
// Deliberately not a provider's control-type list: a play should not have to know that Windows
// calls something a ListItem. It narrows a search and it disambiguates, because a word shared by
// a button and a caption is only one thing you can press.
//
// Empty Kind means "whatever it is", which is the honest answer when the demonstration could not
// tell, and it is treated as saying nothing rather than as excluding anything.
the Target is a set.
this's Name is a text.  // what the person would call it
this's Kind is a text.  // button | item | field | menu | link | tab | checkbox | window
